# Mechanism: from-uv-project — LOCKED uv projects via `uv sync --frozen`.
# Realizer: src/Core.TypeScript/ace/setup-realizers/from-uv-project.ts
#
# Format per line:
#   <repo-relative-project-dir>  opt-in=<ENV_VAR>  [tier=slim|standard|full]
#
# `opt-in=` is REQUIRED. A row without it is refused rather than defaulted to
# "install everywhere" — every project reachable through this mechanism carries
# a large native closure, and a heavy lane that installs by default is how a CI
# minute budget disappears.
#
# NOT `opt-in=ZETA_INSTALL_FULL`, and the reason is measured rather than
# stylistic: `ZETA_INSTALL_FULL=1` is set by three workflows
# (macos-install-sh-test.yml, tlaps-proof.yml, wsl-install-sh-test.yml). Those
# jobs exist to test the INSTALLER; folding a 111-192 MB torch download into
# them would spend the download on three jobs that never read an activation.
# A dedicated flag keeps the cost with the lane that wants it.
#
# `src/Core.Python` and `src/Arc.Python` are deliberately ABSENT. They are real
# locked uv projects and would fit this mechanism, but they are synced today by
# explicit steps in gate.yml / arc-lane.yml, and moving them here would add
# their cost to every install.sh run. Migrating them is a separate decision with
# its own cost case — see docs/INSTALLED.md.

# The model-interpretability lane: activation access (residual-stream reads and
# causal writes) for docs/research/2026-08-23-measuring-latent-geometry-*.md.
# tier=standard because torch needs real RAM; the 8 GB low-memory lane must not
# try. Unblocks work-item 081M0QD23P3087G0R002NQ8217.
src/Interp.Python  opt-in=ZETA_INSTALL_INTERP  tier=standard
